From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:40:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RequestServer+LibWeb: Uncloak CNAMEs for the content blocker

Trackers hide behind first-party names: metrics.example.com is a CNAME
for a tracker's host, so filters for the tracker never see it. uBlock
Origin uncloaks these on Firefox, and Helium patches Chromium's DNS API
so its fork can do the same.

A request can now ask RequestServer to check where its host's aliases
lead. When DNS shows the host to be an alias of another name,
RequestServer tells the client and waits before connecting. WebContent
asks its content blocker, which follows uBlock Origin's defaults: only
requests that no filter matched are uncloaked, aliases within the same
site and requests to the page's own host are left alone, the uncloaked
check uses the alias target's origin, and $cname exceptions apply. A
blocked request fails with a new BlockedByContentBlocker network error.

Only requests with content blocking on, whose host turns out to be an
alias, pay the round trip. The filters stay in WebContent, so
RequestServer does not keep a copy per site, and the per-site toggle
applies as for any other request.

DNS from a configured server already carries the CNAME chain. For the
system resolver, which only gives addresses, LibCore can now ask
getaddrinfo for the canonical name (AI_CANONNAME), and LibDNS records
it as a one-step CNAME.
